      <section class="section section-alt">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="portfolio-grid case-study-layout">
            <article class="content-panel">
              <span class="section-kicker">Project Overview</span>
              <h2>What the system does and why teams depended on it</h2>
              <p>MyHR is a saas platform built for growing organizations that need a single system for daily people operations. The product was designed to replace scattered HR records and approval processes with a structured SaaS workflow that scales with headcount.</p>
              <p>From an engineering perspective, the system had to support frequent attendance activity, employee record access, and approval workflows across multiple organizations without losing trust. That made it a production platform with real operational demands rather than a portfolio demo.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="content-panel">
              <span class="section-kicker">Business Challenge</span>
              <h2>What had to improve for the product to scale cleanly</h2>
              <p>The core business challenge was delivering a product that could serve HR technology and business operations software use cases while still remaining easy for operators to trust and use every day.</p>
              <p>The client wanted to reduce administrative overhead while giving HR teams, managers, and employees a more dependable operating system for routine workforce tasks.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading compact-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Constraints &amp; Complexity</span>
              <h3>Engineering realities that shaped implementation choices</h3>
            </div>
            <p>The delivery had to solve product problems, operational risk, and scaling concerns at the same time.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="expertise-grid project-complexity-grid">
            
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Organization Separation</h3>
              <p>HR data needed clear workspace boundaries across multiple client environments.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Daily Task Volume</h3>
              <p>Attendance and approval actions happen frequently and cannot feel slow or fragile.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Document Security</h3>
              <p>Employee assets and records required secure handling and durable storage.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Workflow Trust</h3>
              <p>Approval and record changes needed traceability for HR teams and managers.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section section-accent">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Solution Architecture</span>
              <h2>System design, integrations, and delivery approach</h2>
            </div>
            <p>The implementation emphasized maintainability, production-readiness, and cleaner operational workflows.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="case-study-hero architecture-layout">
            <article class="content-panel">
              <h3>Implementation approach</h3>
              <p>The solution used Laravel 8, Blade, AWS S3, Stripe, Redis, Google APIs, MySQL across a modular application architecture. Core workflows were separated into clearer domain boundaries so business logic, integrations, and UI concerns did not become tightly coupled.</p>
              <p>That approach made the system easier to scale, easier to monitor, and easier to extend as new requirements appeared. It also created better release confidence for a product expected to handle real customer traffic and operator activity.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="content-panel architecture-diagram-panel">
              <span class="eyebrow">Architecture Diagram</span>
              <div class="architecture-diagram" aria-label="System architecture">
                <div class="arch-node">Operator UI</div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-node">Domain Services</div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-row">
                  <div class="arch-node small">Integrations</div>
                  <div class="arch-node small">Reporting</div>
                </div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-node">Primary Data Store</div>
              </div>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
